---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const faqJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What counts as extra hot chilli?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Extra hot chilli is over 50,000 Scoville Heat Units (SHU) — intense, whole-mouth heat for extreme-heat products, chilli oil and Sichuan-style dishes. Representative varieties are Jixin (50,000–70,000 SHU), Xiaomi, Chilli King and Shuan Shuan."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is extra hot chilli used for?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Extra hot chilli is used where heat is the headline: extreme-heat sauces, chilli oil, Sichuan and Hunan cuisine, and products marketed on their Scoville rating."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Which variety gives the most heat?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Jixin (chicken-heart) pepper is among the hottest common Chinese chillies at 50,000–70,000 SHU, alongside Xiaomi and Chilli King. These are the standard for extreme-heat products and chilli oil."
      }
    }
  ]
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Extra Hot Chilli (50,000+ SHU) | Tenda Peppers"
  description="Extra hot chilli (50,000+ SHU) — Jixin, Xiaomi and Chilli King for extreme heat, chilli oil and Sichuan dishes. China manufacturer, ISO 9001 & HACCP."
  jsonLd={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <div class="container section">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" style="font-size:0.9rem;color:#6b5a54;margin-bottom:16px;">
      <a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/products/dried-chillies/">Dried Chillies</a> &rsaquo; Extra Hot
    </nav>
    <h1>Extra Hot Chilli (50,000+ SHU)</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Intense, whole-mouth heat — the 50,000+ SHU band is for products where heat is the headline,
      built on varieties like Jixin (chicken-heart), Xiaomi and Chilli King.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Extra hot is the 50,000+ SHU band — for products where heat is the headline, not a supporting note. Because heat is non-linear, these varieties are dosed as an accent by weight: a small amount moves a blend's heat far more than the same weight of a medium chilli.
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What counts as extra hot chilli?</h2>
    <p>
      Extra hot is the top of the common Chinese chilli range: over 50,000 SHU. Because heat is
      non-linear, a small dose of extra-hot chilli moves a blend's heat far more than the same weight
      of a high-heat variety — which is why it is specified carefully and usually used as an
      accent, not a base.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Variety</th><th>Heat (SHU)</th><th>Region</th><th>Best for</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Jixin (chicken-heart)</strong></td><td>50,000 – 70,000</td><td>Yunnan, Guizhou</td><td>Extreme heat, Sichuan dishes</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Xiaomi</strong></td><td>30,000 – 50,000</td><td>Yunnan</td><td>Chilli oil, extreme heat</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Chilli King</strong></td><td>&gt;50,000</td><td>Fujian, Hainan</td><td>Extreme-heat products</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Shuan Shuan</strong></td><td>&gt;50,000</td><td>South China</td><td>Extreme-heat seasoning</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What is extra hot chilli used for?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Extreme-heat sauces and chilli oil</li>
      <li>Sichuan and Hunan cuisine</li>
      <li>Products marketed on Scoville rating</li>
      <li>Heat-dosed industrial seasoning</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">How to specify extra hot chilli</h2>
    <p>
      Name the variety and a precise SHU window — Jixin (50,000–70,000 SHU) is the reference for
      extreme heat. Confirm the form (segments and powder are most common at this heat level) and
      dose carefully: extra-hot varieties are usually blended, not used neat.
    </p>
    <p class="data-note">
      Heat values are representative per variety. Confirm your exact SHU window and form when you
      inquire.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What counts as extra hot chilli?</h3>
      <p>Extra hot chilli is over 50,000 Scoville Heat Units (SHU) — intense, whole-mouth heat for extreme-heat products, chilli oil and Sichuan-style dishes. Representative varieties are Jixin (50,000–70,000 SHU), Xiaomi, Chilli King and Shuan Shuan.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is extra hot chilli used for?</h3>
      <p>Extra hot chilli is used where heat is the headline: extreme-heat sauces, chilli oil, Sichuan and Hunan cuisine, and products marketed on their Scoville rating.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Which variety gives the most heat?</h3>
      <p>Jixin (chicken-heart) pepper is among the hottest common Chinese chillies at 50,000–70,000 SHU, alongside Xiaomi and Chilli King. These are the standard for extreme-heat products and chilli oil.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top:32px;">
      <a class="cta" href="/contact/">Request Extra Hot Quotation</a>
      <a class="cta cta-outline" href="/heat-levels/high-heat/" style="margin-left:8px;">← High Heat</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
